I’ve had a look at it.

9
00:00:09,340 --> 00:00:09,642
Bella:

10
00:00:09,642 --> 00:00:13,260
So do you think it’s a good topic for our presentation?

11
00:00:13,260 --> 00:00:13,783
Don:

12
00:00:13,783 --> 00:00:19,010
Well, before I started reading it, I thought recycling footwear.

13
00:00:19,010 --> 00:00:19,340
Don:

14
00:00:19,340 --> 00:00:24,625
well, although it’s quite interesting, perhaps there isn’t enough to say about it, cos

15
00:00:24,625 --> 00:00:28,258
we put shoes in recycling bins, they go to charity shops

16
00:00:28,258 --> 00:00:29,910
and that’s about it.

17
00:00:29,910 --> 00:00:30,153
Bella:

18
00:00:30,153 --> 00:00:32,340
but there’s much more to it than that.

19
00:00:32,340 --> 00:00:32,650
Don:

20
00:00:32,650 --> 00:00:36,680
I realise that now and I’m keen to research the topic more.

21
00:00:36,680 --> 00:00:36,965
Bella:

22
00:00:36,965 --> 00:00:37,820
That’s great.

23
00:00:37,820 --> 00:00:38,139
Don:

24
00:00:38,139 --> 00:00:42,600
One of the things I didn't realise until I read the article was just

25
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:44,830
how many pairs of trainers get recycled!

26
00:00:44,830 --> 00:00:45,173
Bella:

27
00:00:45,173 --> 00:00:49,293
Well, a lot of young people wear them all the time now.

28
00:00:49,293 --> 00:00:52,040
They’ve become more popular than ordinary shoes.

29
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:52,357
Don:

30
00:00:52,357 --> 00:00:52,992
I know.

31
00:00:52,992 --> 00:00:54,895
I guess they are very hard-wearing,

32
00:00:54,895 --> 00:00:58,385
but Don’t they look a bit casual for school uniform?

33
00:00:58,385 --> 00:01:01,240
I Don’t think they’re right for that.

34
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:01,598
Bella:

35
00:01:01,598 --> 00:01:05,542
Actually, I think some of them look quite smart on pupils .

36
00:01:05,542 --> 00:01:05,592
.

37
00:01:05,592 --> 00:01:05,642
.

38
00:01:05,642 --> 00:01:08,410
better than a scruffy old pair of shoes.

39
00:01:08,410 --> 00:01:11,770
Bella:So do you keep shoes a long time?

40
00:01:11,770 --> 00:01:12,134
Don:

41
00:01:12,134 --> 00:01:17,597
Yes.Though I do tend to wear my old pairs for doing dirty jobs like

42
00:01:17,597 --> 00:01:18,690
cleaning my bike.

43
00:01:18,690 --> 00:01:19,009
Bella:

44
00:01:19,009 --> 00:01:22,196
I must admit, I’ve recycled some perfectly good shoes,

45
00:01:22,196 --> 00:01:25,701
that haven’t gone out of fashion and still fit, just

46
00:01:25,701 --> 00:01:28,888
because they don’t look great on me any more.

47
00:01:28,888 --> 00:01:30,800
That’s awful isn’t it?

48
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:31,178
Don:

49
00:01:31,178 --> 00:01:35,330
I think it’s common because there’s so much choice.

50
00:01:35,330 --> 00:01:39,860
The article did say that recent sales of footwear have increased enormously.

51
00:01:39,860 --> 00:01:40,093
Bella:

52
00:01:40,093 --> 00:01:41,260
That didn’t surprise me.

53
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:41,644
Don:

54
00:01:41,644 --> 00:01:42,027
No.

55
00:01:42,027 --> 00:01:46,629
But then it said that the amount of recycled footwear has fallen:

56
00:01:46,629 --> 00:01:51,615
it’s 6 percent now compared to a previous level of 11 percent.

57
00:01:51,615 --> 00:01:54,300
That doesn’t seem to make sense.

58
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:54,714
Bella:

59
00:01:54,714 --> 00:01:58,850
That’s because not everything goes through the recycling process.

60
00:01:58,850 --> 00:01:59,259
Bella:

61
00:01:59,259 --> 00:02:04,983
Some footwear just isn’t good enough to re-sell, for one reason or another,

62
00:02:04,983 --> 00:02:06,210
and gets rejected.

63
00:02:06,210 --> 00:02:06,602
Bella:

64
00:02:06,602 --> 00:02:12,488
So let’s find some examples in the article of footwear that was rejected for

65
00:02:12,488 --> 00:02:12,880
recycling.

66
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:13,334
Don:

67
00:02:13,334 --> 00:02:13,788
OK.

68
00:02:13,788 --> 00:02:19,234
I think there are some in the interview with the recycling manager.

69
00:02:19,234 --> 00:02:21,050
Yeah – here it is.

70
00:02:21,050 --> 00:02:21,514
Bella:

71
00:02:21,514 --> 00:02:21,979
Mmm.

72
00:02:21,979 --> 00:02:25,694
Let’s start with the ladies’ high-heeled shoes.

73
00:02:25,694 --> 00:02:28,480
What did he say about those?

74
00:02:28,480 --> 00:02:28,966
Don:

75
00:02:28,966 --> 00:02:33,831
He said they were probably expensive – the material was suede

76
00:02:33,831 --> 00:02:36,750
and they were beige in colour,

77
00:02:36,750 --> 00:02:40,222
Don:it looked like someone had only worn them once,

78
00:02:40,222 --> 00:02:45,084
but in a very wet field so the heels were too stained with mud

79
00:02:45,084 --> 00:02:46,820
and grass to re-sell them.

80
00:02:46,820 --> 00:02:47,292
Bella:

81
00:02:47,292 --> 00:02:47,765
OK .

82
00:02:47,765 --> 00:02:47,815
.

83
00:02:47,815 --> 00:02:47,865
.

84
00:02:47,865 --> 00:02:50,128
and the leather ankle boots.

85
00:02:50,128 --> 00:02:52,490
What was wrong with them?

86
00:02:52,490 --> 00:02:52,838
Don:

87
00:02:52,838 --> 00:02:56,661
Apparently, the heels were worn – but that wasn’t the problem.

88
00:02:56,661 --> 00:03:01,179
One of the shoes was a much lighter shade than the other one –

89
00:03:01,179 --> 00:03:03,960
it had obviously been left in the sun.

90
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:04,281
Don:

91
00:03:04,281 --> 00:03:07,170
I suppose even second-hand shoes should look the same!

92
00:03:07,170 --> 00:03:07,677
Bella:

93
00:03:07,677 --> 00:03:08,183
Sure.

94
00:03:08,183 --> 00:03:11,730
Then there were the red baby shoes.

95
00:03:11,730 --> 00:03:12,080
Don:

96
00:03:12,080 --> 00:03:16,974
Oh yes – we’re told to tie shoes together when we put them in

97
00:03:16,974 --> 00:03:18,022
a recycling bin,

98
00:03:18,022 --> 00:03:20,120
but people often don’t bother.

99
00:03:20,120 --> 00:03:20,432
Bella:

100
00:03:20,432 --> 00:03:24,180
You’d think it would have been easy to find the other,

101
00:03:24,180 --> 00:03:25,429
but it wasn’t.

102
00:03:25,429 --> 00:03:28,240
That was a shame because they were obviously new.

103
00:03:28,240 --> 00:03:28,661
Don:

104
00:03:28,661 --> 00:03:30,346
The trainers were interesting.

105
00:03:30,346 --> 00:03:35,820
He said they looked like they’d been worn by a marathon runner.

106
00:03:35,820 --> 00:03:36,292
Bella:

107
00:03:36,292 --> 00:03:38,650
Yeah – weren’t they split?

108
00:03:38,650 --> 00:03:38,970
Don:

109
00:03:38,970 --> 00:03:39,609
Not exactly.

110
00:03:39,609 --> 00:03:44,082
One of the soles was so worn under the foot that you could put

111
00:03:44,082 --> 00:03:45,360
